[129.57831, "o", "\r\u001b[2K\r\n\u001b[1;31mmini-swe-agent\u001b[0m\u001b[31m (step \u001b[0m\u001b[1;31m8\u001b[0m\u001b[31m, \u001b[0m\u001b[1;31m$0.00\u001b[0m\u001b[31m):\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[32m⠼\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond..."]
[129.578929, "o", "\r\u001b[2KThe diagnostics confirm only `ComputerScience_NTUA` and `HistoryAndPhilosophy_NKUA` qualify among EU departments (12 current students each); Berkeley also has \r\n11 but is in the US. I’ll make the aggregation robust to identical professor names by grouping on both the professor resource and name, then re-run the final \r\nquery.\r\n\r\n```\r\nsed -i 's/^GROUP BY ?professorName$/GROUP BY ?professor ?professorName/' /app/solution.sparql\r\n/tmp/sparql-venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\r\nfrom rdflib import Graph\r\ng = Graph()\r\ng.parse('/app/university_graph.ttl', format='turtle')\r\nq = open('/app/solution.sparql').read()\r\nrows = list(g.query(q))\r\nprint('Rows:', len(rows))\r\n"]
[129.578957, "o", "for name, countries in rows:\r\n    print(f'{name}: {countries}')\r\nPY\r\nnl -ba /app/solution.sparql | tail -n 8\r\n```\r\n\u001b[32m⠼\u001b[0m Waiting for the LM to respond..."]
